1
00:00:02,620 --> 00:00:04,210
Look around the room you're in.

2
00:00:04,405 --> 00:00:05,690
Now turn your head.

3
00:00:07,620 --> 00:00:10,250
The chair you just saw has
left your field of view.

4
00:00:10,420 --> 00:00:12,110
But it hasn't left your mind.

5
00:00:12,487 --> 00:00:14,117
You could still point to it.

6
00:00:14,287 --> 00:00:17,267
You could guess what you would
see after a step to the left.

7
00:00:17,437 --> 00:00:21,247
And if someone asked you to walk over
to it, you would know which way to go.

8
00:00:21,620 --> 00:00:24,830
This internal picture,
of where things are relative to you,

9
00:00:24,850 --> 00:00:29,300
including things you can't currently see,
is what we will call a spatial state.

10
00:00:30,487 --> 00:00:35,527
Modern visual foundation models learn
from enormous amounts of images and video.

11
00:00:35,697 --> 00:00:37,872
They estimate depth, track objects,

12
00:00:37,892 --> 00:00:40,937
and even generate convincing
camera fly-throughs.

13
00:00:41,345 --> 00:00:42,990
So here is the question.

14
00:00:43,160 --> 00:00:47,450
Do their internal representations
support a spatial state like this one?

15
00:00:48,320 --> 00:00:50,220
Let's find out, by probing them.

16
00:00:55,520 --> 00:00:58,520
The idea has deep roots
in the study of perception.

17
00:00:58,887 --> 00:01:03,602
Object-file theories describe how information
stays attached to the same object,

18
00:01:03,622 --> 00:01:05,637
even as its appearance changes.

19
00:01:06,003 --> 00:01:09,688
Path integration describes how an
animal keeps track of where it is,

20
00:01:09,708 --> 00:01:11,793
by adding up its own movements.

21
00:01:12,153 --> 00:01:15,243
And sensorimotor theories
argue that to perceive is

22
00:01:15,263 --> 00:01:17,953
to know how your input
would change if you moved.

23
00:01:18,355 --> 00:01:22,935
So, an observer's spatial state is the
configuration of the surroundings relative

24
00:01:22,955 --> 00:01:27,880
to the observer, including objects seen
earlier that are no longer visible.

25
00:01:28,050 --> 00:01:31,885
A spatial state representation
is an internal encoding of it,

26
00:01:31,905 --> 00:01:33,920
inferred from visual history.

27
00:01:34,313 --> 00:01:37,868
Notice what this does not assume:
no explicit 3D map,

28
00:01:37,888 --> 00:01:40,813
no scene graph, no particular architecture.

29
00:01:40,983 --> 00:01:45,503
Instead, the state is characterized
by what it lets you do, at four levels.

30
00:01:45,892 --> 00:01:48,037
Level one, spatial structure.

31
00:01:48,207 --> 00:01:52,162
Can you read out the geometry
of what you see: depth, camera pose,

32
00:01:52,182 --> 00:01:56,127
which pixels belong to which object,
and how two views correspond?

33
00:01:56,503 --> 00:01:58,783
Level two, object persistence.

34
00:01:58,953 --> 00:02:02,793
When an object leaves the field of view,
can you still say where it is?

35
00:02:03,170 --> 00:02:05,860
Level three, predictive spatial update.

36
00:02:06,030 --> 00:02:09,115
When the camera moves,
can you anticipate what you will see,

37
00:02:09,135 --> 00:02:12,880
in one direct jump,
or across a whole sequence of steps?

38
00:02:13,253 --> 00:02:15,303
Level four, actionability.

39
00:02:15,473 --> 00:02:17,258
Given a goal, can you use the state

40
00:02:17,278 --> 00:02:19,993
to pick an action that
actually brings you closer?

41
00:02:20,370 --> 00:02:22,730
The levels climb from
reading the current scene,

42
00:02:22,750 --> 00:02:25,850
to retaining it,
to updating it, to acting on it.

43
00:02:26,020 --> 00:02:30,230
And acting closes the loop:
every action changes what you see next.

44
00:02:32,353 --> 00:02:34,533
How do you ask a network what it knows?

45
00:02:34,713 --> 00:02:35,873
You probe it.

46
00:02:36,237 --> 00:02:38,667
Freeze the foundation model completely.

47
00:02:38,837 --> 00:02:42,577
Pass video through it, and take the
features from one of its layers.

48
00:02:42,747 --> 00:02:45,532
Then train a small readout network, the probe,

49
00:02:45,552 --> 00:02:48,317
to predict a spatial target
from those features.

50
00:02:48,670 --> 00:02:50,955
If a light probe can recover the target,

51
00:02:50,975 --> 00:02:54,190
that information is accessible
in the representation.

52
00:02:54,360 --> 00:02:56,230
The backbone never changes.

53
00:02:56,400 --> 00:02:57,970
Only the probe learns.

54
00:02:58,337 --> 00:02:59,877
The setting is streaming.

55
00:03:00,047 --> 00:03:04,047
The observer sees past and present frames,
but never the future.

56
00:03:04,403 --> 00:03:08,526
For model m and layer l,
the encoder processes a causal prefix:

57
00:03:08,546 --> 00:03:13,293
frames one through p,
where p is 8, 12, 16, or 24.

58
00:03:13,463 --> 00:03:15,673
Each prefix is encoded on its own,

59
00:03:15,693 --> 00:03:18,833
and each one is treated as
a candidate spatial state.

60
00:03:19,203 --> 00:03:22,323
Prediction and action
also need a future frame.

61
00:03:22,493 --> 00:03:26,293
That frame, h steps ahead,
is encoded in isolation,

62
00:03:26,313 --> 00:03:30,723
with no access to the prefix,
so the answer never leaks into the input.

63
00:03:31,087 --> 00:03:33,527
And because knowledge may
live at different depths,

64
00:03:33,547 --> 00:03:35,852
and may depend on how much has been seen,

65
00:03:35,872 --> 00:03:41,667
the analyses are organized along two axes:
network depth, and streaming context.

66
00:03:42,353 --> 00:03:45,803
Ten frozen representations go
through this pipeline.

67
00:03:46,170 --> 00:03:48,470
An image encoder, DINOv2.

68
00:03:48,640 --> 00:03:52,140
Two video encoders, V-JEPA 2 and VideoMAE.

69
00:03:52,310 --> 00:03:55,430
And a 3D reconstruction model, Fast3R.

70
00:03:55,787 --> 00:04:02,337
Four video generators: Wan 2.1,
CogVideoX, MiniMax-H3, and LingBot-video.

71
00:04:02,507 --> 00:04:04,217
A world model, Aether.

72
00:04:04,387 --> 00:04:08,017
And a multimodal language model, Qwen2.5-VL.

73
00:04:08,387 --> 00:04:12,540
All of them watch the same thing:
camera trajectories with known poses,

74
00:04:12,560 --> 00:04:15,917
through real indoor scenes from ScanNet++.

75
00:04:18,037 --> 00:04:22,687
Let's open up the probes, one level at a time,
starting with spatial structure.

76
00:04:23,053 --> 00:04:25,313
The geometry probe is a small transformer

77
00:04:25,333 --> 00:04:27,513
that alternates attention within each frame

78
00:04:27,533 --> 00:04:33,043
and across frames, followed by dense
prediction heads, in the style of VGGT.

79
00:04:33,408 --> 00:04:35,913
One head predicts depth at every pixel.

80
00:04:36,083 --> 00:04:37,986
A camera head predicts the pose,

81
00:04:38,006 --> 00:04:43,293
scored by the area under the accuracy
curve up to thirty degrees: AUC at 30.

82
00:04:43,653 --> 00:04:46,633
A third head maps every pixel to an embedding.

83
00:04:46,803 --> 00:04:51,401
Each pixel is classified against the mean
embedding of every ground-truth object,

84
00:04:51,421 --> 00:04:55,203
so a low loss means instances
are cleanly separated.

85
00:04:55,373 --> 00:04:58,833
This measures identity separation,
not mask quality.

86
00:04:59,208 --> 00:05:02,013
View consistency asks a different question.

87
00:05:02,183 --> 00:05:04,948
Each view is pooled into a single vector,

88
00:05:04,968 --> 00:05:08,173
and the probe compares two views
through their concatenation,

89
00:05:08,193 --> 00:05:10,273
their product, and their difference,

90
00:05:10,293 --> 00:05:13,523
to decide whether the two views
share a region of the scene.

91
00:05:15,637 --> 00:05:18,637
Object persistence is
where things get interesting.

92
00:05:19,003 --> 00:05:21,593
A target object is seen early in the clip.

93
00:05:21,763 --> 00:05:25,733
We pool the features inside its
mask into a single query vector:

94
00:05:25,753 --> 00:05:30,273
an appearance signature that carries
no location, and no camera pose.

95
00:05:30,653 --> 00:05:35,243
By the end of the prefix, the camera has
turned, and the object is out of view.

96
00:05:35,953 --> 00:05:39,008
The probe adds the query
to a learnable belief token,

97
00:05:39,028 --> 00:05:42,258
places it next to every
patch token from every frame,

98
00:05:42,278 --> 00:05:44,813
and runs self-attention over all of them.

99
00:05:45,187 --> 00:05:50,335
The belief token then votes over 16
azimuth bins and 8 elevation bins,

100
00:05:50,355 --> 00:05:54,837
a sphere of directions around the final
camera, and regresses the distance.

101
00:05:55,220 --> 00:05:57,275
The score is the angle between the predicted

102
00:05:57,295 --> 00:06:00,180
and the true direction, so lower is better.

103
00:06:00,350 --> 00:06:04,640
And the probe is never told where the
object was, or how the camera moved.

104
00:06:06,770 --> 00:06:10,070
In direct prediction,
the probe receives the current features,

105
00:06:10,090 --> 00:06:13,707
plus the camera motion to a
future frame: nine numbers,

106
00:06:13,727 --> 00:06:16,640
six for rotation and three for translation.

107
00:06:16,810 --> 00:06:21,730
In a single update, it predicts that frame's
feature, one, two, or four steps ahead.

108
00:06:22,087 --> 00:06:23,957
Path integration is harder.

109
00:06:24,127 --> 00:06:26,772
A recurrent updater starts
from the current state,

110
00:06:26,792 --> 00:06:31,487
applies the motions one after another,
and then decodes where it has ended up.

111
00:06:31,692 --> 00:06:34,067
Small errors at each step can compound.

112
00:06:34,437 --> 00:06:36,457
How do you score a predicted feature?

113
00:06:36,627 --> 00:06:37,837
By retrieval.

114
00:06:38,017 --> 00:06:41,585
Line up the true future features
of many samples, and ask:

115
00:06:41,605 --> 00:06:45,127
is the closest one,
by cosine similarity, the right one?

116
00:06:45,487 --> 00:06:47,877
The fraction of hits is recall at one.

117
00:06:48,047 --> 00:06:50,772
Each model is scored in its own feature space,

118
00:06:50,792 --> 00:06:53,607
so these numbers compare models within a task.

119
00:06:53,777 --> 00:06:56,167
They are not a shared physical error scale.

120
00:06:58,302 --> 00:07:01,937
So what happens when all ten
models face all seven measurements?

121
00:07:02,303 --> 00:07:04,863
Lesson one: there is no single winner.

122
00:07:05,220 --> 00:07:09,999
Fast3R, trained for 3D reconstruction,
reads camera pose far better

123
00:07:10,020 --> 00:07:17,230
than anything else: an AUC of 0.39,
against 0.04 for DINOv2.

124
00:07:17,400 --> 00:07:21,040
Yet DINOv2 separates object instances better.

125
00:07:21,210 --> 00:07:26,010
One caveat: Fast3R was
trained partly on ScanNet++,

126
00:07:26,030 --> 00:07:28,310
including some of these validation scenes,

127
00:07:28,330 --> 00:07:31,010
so part of its lead may come from familiarity.

128
00:07:31,390 --> 00:07:33,410
Now look at object persistence.

129
00:07:33,580 --> 00:07:39,830
Fast3R leads again, at 32.8 degrees
of error, with VideoMAE close behind.

130
00:07:39,999 --> 00:07:45,040
And VideoMAE locates hidden objects
far better than DINOv2 or Qwen,

131
00:07:45,060 --> 00:07:49,790
even though both beat it on depth,
instance identity, and view consistency.

132
00:07:49,985 --> 00:07:53,050
Seeing a scene well does not
guarantee remembering it.

133
00:07:53,435 --> 00:07:55,410
So what does track persistence?

134
00:07:55,580 --> 00:07:56,880
Camera pose.

135
00:07:57,050 --> 00:08:02,243
The three best localizers,
Fast3R, VideoMAE, and V-JEPA 2,

136
00:08:02,263 --> 00:08:05,610
are exactly the three models
with the highest pose AUC.

137
00:08:05,780 --> 00:08:10,338
Across all ten models,
the rank correlation is 0.76,

138
00:08:10,358 --> 00:08:15,060
against at most 0.43 for depth,
instance identity, or view.

139
00:08:15,230 --> 00:08:17,820
And in all four backbones with layer sweeps,

140
00:08:17,840 --> 00:08:21,650
persistence peaks at the very layer
where metric geometry peaks.

141
00:08:22,020 --> 00:08:24,900
Turn to prediction,
and the order changes again.

142
00:08:25,070 --> 00:08:29,875
DINOv2 leads direct prediction,
V-JEPA 2 leads path integration,

143
00:08:29,895 --> 00:08:34,475
and Qwen comes next on both,
level with DINOv2 on path integration,

144
00:08:34,495 --> 00:08:36,590
despite its weak persistence.

145
00:08:36,953 --> 00:08:40,673
The two prediction tasks broadly agree,
but their leaders swap.

146
00:08:40,843 --> 00:08:47,298
DINOv2 beats V-JEPA 2
on direct prediction, 0.24 to 0.06,

147
00:08:47,318 --> 00:08:53,023
yet V-JEPA 2 wins path
integration, 0.16 to 0.09.

148
00:08:53,193 --> 00:08:56,013
And neither ordering tracks
the persistence ordering.

149
00:08:56,370 --> 00:08:59,120
And model families don't decide the profile.

150
00:08:59,290 --> 00:09:04,960
VideoMAE and V-JEPA 2 are both video
encoders with similar view consistency.

151
00:09:05,130 --> 00:09:10,660
Yet VideoMAE is better on camera pose
and persistence, while V-JEPA 2 is better

152
00:09:10,680 --> 00:09:12,810
on depth and path integration,

153
00:09:12,830 --> 00:09:16,210
and on direct prediction the
two are statistically tied.

154
00:09:16,380 --> 00:09:18,980
A single spatial score would hide all of this.

155
00:09:21,087 --> 00:09:23,837
Lesson two: where, and when, you look matters.

156
00:09:24,238 --> 00:09:27,993
Sweep through the layers, and different
readouts peak at different depths.

157
00:09:28,163 --> 00:09:32,388
In V-JEPA 2, prediction and instance
identity peak at the last block,

158
00:09:32,408 --> 00:09:37,083
23, but object persistence
peaks earlier, at block 17.

159
00:09:37,442 --> 00:09:41,109
VideoMAE flips this:
persistence and identity peak

160
00:09:41,129 --> 00:09:44,467
at its last block, and prediction at block 17.

161
00:09:44,637 --> 00:09:50,037
The Wan generator peaks earlier still,
at block 15, for five of its six readouts.

162
00:09:50,428 --> 00:09:53,608
So a layer chosen for one
task need not serve another,

163
00:09:53,628 --> 00:09:55,623
and even the ranking can change.

164
00:09:55,793 --> 00:09:58,092
With an 8-frame prefix at block 17,

165
00:09:58,112 --> 00:10:02,700
V-JEPA 2 reaches 28.8 degrees
of localization error,

166
00:10:02,720 --> 00:10:05,825
just below VideoMAE's best setting,

167
00:10:05,845 --> 00:10:10,323
although the main table puts
VideoMAE ahead by about 9 degrees.

168
00:10:10,692 --> 00:10:12,417
Now vary the context.

169
00:10:12,587 --> 00:10:15,277
You might expect more history to always help.

170
00:10:15,447 --> 00:10:16,517
It doesn't.

171
00:10:16,887 --> 00:10:20,644
Comparing a 24-frame prefix
with an 8-frame prefix,

172
00:10:20,664 --> 00:10:26,227
51 of the 63 paired comparisons,
81 percent, favor the shorter one.

173
00:10:26,397 --> 00:10:30,697
All nine object persistence pairs
get worse with the longer prefix,

174
00:10:30,717 --> 00:10:34,077
and path integration worsens
for seven of the nine models.

175
00:10:34,437 --> 00:10:40,242
VideoMAE's localization error holds
near 30 degrees up to 16 frames,

176
00:10:40,262 --> 00:10:42,717
then jumps to 55 at 24.

177
00:10:42,887 --> 00:10:49,027
DINOv2's path integration
recall drops from 0.13 to 0.07.

178
00:10:49,197 --> 00:10:53,417
Fast3R, by contrast,
improves on depth and camera pose.

179
00:10:53,787 --> 00:10:57,462
One caveat: a longer prefix
also moves the endpoint,

180
00:10:57,482 --> 00:11:00,187
so this does not isolate history alone.

181
00:11:00,367 --> 00:11:03,722
Still, the extraction setting
is part of the evaluation,

182
00:11:03,742 --> 00:11:05,797
not an interchangeable detail.

183
00:11:07,903 --> 00:11:09,603
Here's a subtler question.

184
00:11:09,773 --> 00:11:12,653
When a probe succeeds,
what is it actually using?

185
00:11:13,020 --> 00:11:15,370
A probe can score well through shortcuts.

186
00:11:15,550 --> 00:11:18,393
So the paper runs controls:
keep the trained probe

187
00:11:18,413 --> 00:11:21,440
and the target fixed,
and change only the input.

188
00:11:21,803 --> 00:11:25,853
First, the simplest way to predict
the next frame: copy the current one.

189
00:11:26,023 --> 00:11:30,953
For the Wan generator, learned prediction
beats copying, 15 percent to 12.

190
00:11:31,123 --> 00:11:36,473
But for V-JEPA 2, copying the last
feature wins: 20 percent, against 13.

191
00:11:36,872 --> 00:11:39,397
Second, does the predictor use the motion?

192
00:11:39,567 --> 00:11:42,434
Feed it the correct camera motion,
or a wrong one,

193
00:11:42,454 --> 00:11:45,207
drawn from candidates
with matched displacement.

194
00:11:45,570 --> 00:11:49,063
Correct motion helps V-JEPA 2
by almost 19 points,

195
00:11:49,083 --> 00:11:53,980
VideoMAE by 10.5, and CogVideoX by about 4.

196
00:11:54,150 --> 00:11:59,080
For the Wan generator, the gain of about 3
points has an interval that crosses zero.

197
00:11:59,437 --> 00:12:01,887
Third, does persistence use the history?

198
00:12:02,057 --> 00:12:07,087
Give the probe the full video, or only the
final frame, with the same object query.

199
00:12:07,453 --> 00:12:11,823
History cuts the error by about 10
degrees for V-JEPA 2, and for Wan.

200
00:12:11,993 --> 00:12:15,938
But for VideoMAE, the most accurate
of these four localizers,

201
00:12:15,958 --> 00:12:19,293
the benefit is small,
and its interval spans zero.

202
00:12:19,668 --> 00:12:23,023
So decodability and input
use are different things.

203
00:12:23,193 --> 00:12:27,883
A probe can be accurate without relying
on the very inputs the task is about.

204
00:12:30,003 --> 00:12:32,188
A related question arises for models

205
00:12:32,208 --> 00:12:35,543
that natively predict the future,
like V-JEPA 2.

206
00:12:35,713 --> 00:12:39,193
Its predictor outputs features
for frames it has not seen.

207
00:12:39,363 --> 00:12:42,543
Do those predictions still
carry readable geometry?

208
00:12:42,903 --> 00:12:47,803
Train a depth reader on real future
features, and its error is 0.18.

209
00:12:48,170 --> 00:12:51,720
Now feed that same frozen reader
the model's own predictions.

210
00:12:51,890 --> 00:12:54,735
The error jumps to about 0.25,

211
00:12:54,755 --> 00:12:59,060
slightly worse than a layout prior
that ignores the input entirely.

212
00:12:59,420 --> 00:13:01,090
Is the geometry gone?

213
00:13:01,270 --> 00:13:02,400
Not quite.

214
00:13:02,570 --> 00:13:06,135
Retrain an equal-capacity reader
directly on the predictions,

215
00:13:06,155 --> 00:13:10,010
and the error drops to 0.22:
better than the prior,

216
00:13:10,030 --> 00:13:12,280
though still short of real features.

217
00:13:12,450 --> 00:13:15,300
Copying the context is worse than all of them.

218
00:13:15,670 --> 00:13:20,400
Prediction keeps recoverable spatial
structure, but it changes the interface.

219
00:13:20,570 --> 00:13:23,080
The original reader no longer fits.

220
00:13:25,197 --> 00:13:26,787
Finally, level four.

221
00:13:26,957 --> 00:13:29,197
Can the representation guide action?

222
00:13:29,553 --> 00:13:31,303
The first test is a choice.

223
00:13:31,473 --> 00:13:35,783
Given the current features, a goal frame,
and two candidate camera motions,

224
00:13:35,803 --> 00:13:38,483
pick the one that makes more
progress toward the goal.

225
00:13:38,930 --> 00:13:42,920
Knowing the actions alone
gets you 67.8 percent.

226
00:13:43,090 --> 00:13:48,085
With visual features, the four tested
backbones reach 76 to 80 percent,

227
00:13:48,105 --> 00:13:51,350
and shuffling the goals costs 12 to 21 points.

228
00:13:51,520 --> 00:13:53,580
The readout clearly uses the goal.

229
00:13:53,937 --> 00:13:58,317
Curiously, first predicting each
candidate's outcome, and then scoring it,

230
00:13:58,337 --> 00:14:04,147
brings no consistent advantage,
and drops Wan's accuracy to 63.6 percent.

231
00:14:04,503 --> 00:14:09,273
The second test removes the candidates:
the probe must output the motion itself.

232
00:14:09,443 --> 00:14:11,698
Current and goal features are projected,

233
00:14:11,718 --> 00:14:15,163
then fused with their difference
and their element-wise product.

234
00:14:15,787 --> 00:14:20,624
This full model makes positive progress
on 50 to 60 percent of trajectories,

235
00:14:20,644 --> 00:14:24,237
beating a goal-only version by 6 to 14 points.

236
00:14:24,417 --> 00:14:26,157
So the current state matters.

237
00:14:26,327 --> 00:14:28,134
But adding the goal doesn't help:

238
00:14:28,154 --> 00:14:31,477
it never beats a current-only
version that ignores the goal.

239
00:14:31,842 --> 00:14:33,887
Now, the most telling experiment.

240
00:14:34,057 --> 00:14:36,597
Keep the current state
and the trained weights fixed,

241
00:14:36,617 --> 00:14:40,447
and swap in a goal from a different scene,
matched for difficulty.

242
00:14:40,803 --> 00:14:44,763
The predicted direction swings
by 30 to 46 degrees.

243
00:14:44,933 --> 00:14:47,343
The model is clearly listening to the goal.

244
00:14:47,703 --> 00:14:49,543
Yet progress barely moves.

245
00:14:49,713 --> 00:14:54,393
The difference between correct
and shuffled goals ranges from minus 2.3

246
00:14:54,413 --> 00:14:59,193
to plus 1.2 points, and every
confidence interval contains zero.

247
00:14:59,553 --> 00:15:03,438
It's like a compass needle that turns
whenever you change the destination,

248
00:15:03,458 --> 00:15:05,093
but doesn't point you toward it.

249
00:15:05,263 --> 00:15:08,053
Goal sensitivity is not goal utility.

250
00:15:10,170 --> 00:15:12,110
How do the four levels relate?

251
00:15:12,280 --> 00:15:14,999
The results point to two
chains of association.

252
00:15:15,370 --> 00:15:19,140
The first links camera pose,
object persistence, and action.

253
00:15:19,310 --> 00:15:24,147
Among the four backbones tested on action,
the two best at pose and persistence,

254
00:15:24,167 --> 00:15:29,060
Fast3R and V-JEPA 2, are also the
two best at choosing between moves,

255
00:15:29,080 --> 00:15:31,830
and Fast3R makes the most progress.

256
00:15:31,999 --> 00:15:34,940
DINOv2, the best predictor, leads neither.

257
00:15:35,303 --> 00:15:37,433
The second links what the scene contains,

258
00:15:37,453 --> 00:15:40,433
its depth, objects, and views, to prediction.

259
00:15:40,603 --> 00:15:44,818
Both prediction tasks rank models much
like these scene-content readouts do,

260
00:15:44,838 --> 00:15:47,703
but not like camera pose or persistence.

261
00:15:48,070 --> 00:15:50,820
Yet strong scene-content
readout is not enough.

262
00:15:50,990 --> 00:15:53,595
Fast3R reads depth and view best,

263
00:15:53,615 --> 00:15:56,410
and still ranks only seventh
of nine at prediction.

264
00:15:56,770 --> 00:15:59,532
In each chain, strong lower-level readout goes

265
00:15:59,552 --> 00:16:02,040
with the higher level without guaranteeing it.

266
00:16:02,210 --> 00:16:06,680
That is why no single diagnostic
can stand in for the whole profile.

267
00:16:08,802 --> 00:16:10,117
Let's step back.

268
00:16:10,287 --> 00:16:12,455
Across ten frozen representations,

269
00:16:12,475 --> 00:16:16,527
spatial readouts shift with the task,
the layer, and the context.

270
00:16:16,707 --> 00:16:18,547
No model leads everywhere.

271
00:16:18,903 --> 00:16:21,093
And three questions keep coming apart.

272
00:16:21,263 --> 00:16:23,103
Is the information readable?

273
00:16:23,273 --> 00:16:26,553
Is it actually used: the motion,
the history, the goal?

274
00:16:26,723 --> 00:16:28,383
And does it help behavior?

275
00:16:28,762 --> 00:16:31,027
Seeing a scene does not mean remembering it.

276
00:16:31,197 --> 00:16:34,557
Predicting features does not
mean preserving how they read.

277
00:16:34,727 --> 00:16:37,627
And responding to a goal
does not mean reaching it.

278
00:16:37,987 --> 00:16:42,127
These are offline, probe-based
diagnostics in static indoor scenes.

279
00:16:42,297 --> 00:16:46,982
Each prefix is encoded on its own,
so the results describe what a representation

280
00:16:47,002 --> 00:16:51,037
of the observed history affords,
not an online state update.

281
00:16:51,403 --> 00:16:52,983
But the message is clear.

282
00:16:53,163 --> 00:16:56,181
To judge whether a visual
model holds a spatial state,

283
00:16:56,201 --> 00:16:58,368
ask not only what can be decoded,

284
00:16:58,388 --> 00:17:03,293
but whether it can be kept beyond view,
updated under motion, and used to act.

